00:05:25.820 --> 00:05:28.130
to learn something
new, so that's great.

00:05:28.130 --> 00:05:31.610
Elixir and Erlang are both
closely related languages.

00:05:31.610 --> 00:05:34.280
They're both functional
programming languages.

00:05:34.280 --> 00:05:36.490
They both target a
platform called OTP.

00:05:39.080 --> 00:05:41.980
Actually, OTP is
part of the platform.

00:05:41.980 --> 00:05:44.510
There's a bunch of
related components.

00:05:44.510 --> 00:05:45.650
There's a VM.

00:05:45.650 --> 00:05:47.600
There is a runtime system.

00:05:47.600 --> 00:05:51.590
OTP relates to the library,
the standard library,

00:05:51.590 --> 00:05:54.970
and frameworks associated
with the platform.

00:05:54.970 --> 00:05:58.360
But it's a really interesting
platform for web development.

00:05:58.360 --> 00:06:01.280
It has powerful features
around concurrency

00:06:01.280 --> 00:06:03.290
and around distributed systems.

00:06:03.290 --> 00:06:05.300
And it also has some
really interesting features

00:06:05.300 --> 00:06:08.540
around fault tolerance
and error recovery.

00:06:08.540 --> 00:06:13.700
But on the flip
side, Erlang and OTP,

00:06:13.700 --> 00:06:17.840
the architecture of an app
written for this platform

00:06:17.840 --> 00:06:21.290
can be different from
the way that we're

00:06:21.290 --> 00:06:25.190
used to writing web
apps in other languages,

00:06:25.190 --> 00:06:29.960
like Node.js, or Ruby,
or Go, or anything else.

00:06:29.960 --> 00:06:33.020
And this difference is something
that I first encountered when

00:06:33.020 --> 00:06:35.900
I was first learning Elixir.

00:06:35.900 --> 00:06:38.990
I had joined a study
group with some friends

00:06:38.990 --> 00:06:40.580
from a local meetup.

00:06:40.580 --> 00:06:42.050
This was about four years ago.

00:06:42.050 --> 00:06:45.560
And we did a book study
in the study group,

00:06:45.560 --> 00:06:47.510
and then four of
us got together,

00:06:47.510 --> 00:06:50.570
and we said, you know,
let's put some of the things

00:06:50.570 --> 00:06:55.310
that we've learned into practice
and do a project together.

00:06:55.310 --> 00:06:56.990
So we did this project.

00:06:56.990 --> 00:07:00.050
This is a screenshot of what
we built. As you can see,

00:07:00.050 --> 00:07:03.050
it's a simple
multiplayer tank game.

00:07:03.050 --> 00:07:06.618
We wrote it as a web
app, and you've probably

00:07:06.618 --> 00:07:07.910
seen this sort of thing before.

00:07:07.910 --> 00:07:09.650
You drive a tank around a maze.

00:07:09.650 --> 00:07:11.720
You have other players
that are online with you,

00:07:11.720 --> 00:07:13.095
and they're driving
around tanks,

00:07:13.095 --> 00:07:14.880
and you try to shoot them down.

00:07:14.880 --> 00:07:16.790
So it's online.

00:07:16.790 --> 00:07:18.280
It's multiplayer.

00:07:18.280 --> 00:07:20.440
It's real-time.

00:07:20.440 --> 00:07:24.830
Now, none of us were
professional game developers.

00:07:24.830 --> 00:07:27.342
This is the first time that
we've done something like this.

00:07:27.342 --> 00:07:29.300
And we're trying to learn
that at the same time

00:07:29.300 --> 00:07:31.920
we're learning Elixir,
so it was a lot of fun.

00:07:31.920 --> 00:07:33.050
It was a challenge.

00:07:33.050 --> 00:07:35.180
But we had to figure
out a bunch of things

00:07:35.180 --> 00:07:37.440
while we were doing this.

00:07:37.440 --> 00:07:41.360
And in particular, we learned
that doing this kind of thing

00:07:41.360 --> 00:07:46.880
as a web app presents
some challenges.

00:07:46.880 --> 00:07:51.300
We figured out early
on that the game logic,

00:07:51.300 --> 00:07:53.360
you know, keeping
the game state,

00:07:53.360 --> 00:07:56.120
tracking the objects
on the screen,

00:07:56.120 --> 00:07:58.670
doing collision
detection, all that stuff,

00:07:58.670 --> 00:08:02.270
it has to be run over
here on the server side

00:08:02.270 --> 00:08:04.610
because we have multiple
players, multiple clients

00:08:04.610 --> 00:08:07.880
connected, so we need some
central source of truth

00:08:07.880 --> 00:08:09.450
for what's going on.

00:08:09.450 --> 00:08:14.880
So that means we have to stream
updates across to the clients.

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:17.038
So this is an action game.

00:08:17.038 --> 00:08:18.080
This is a real-time game.

00:08:18.080 --> 00:08:22.720
We want 20, 30 frames per
second, so REST requests,

00:08:22.720 --> 00:08:27.200
HTTP, not going to
work, too much latency.

00:08:27.200 --> 00:08:30.410
So we changed that
to a WebSocket.

00:08:30.410 --> 00:08:35.059
And in general, we started
to tinker with this web app

00:08:35.059 --> 00:08:36.110
architecture.

00:08:36.110 --> 00:08:37.640
We started to
change things around

00:08:37.640 --> 00:08:40.610
because of what
our use case was,

00:08:40.610 --> 00:08:42.409
what our requirements were.

00:08:42.409 --> 00:08:44.030
We looked at the
game logic itself,

00:08:44.030 --> 00:08:49.280
and we realized, OK, this is
a long-running game simulation

00:08:49.280 --> 00:08:50.660
that we're running here.

00:08:50.660 --> 00:08:53.660
This is a background process
that's continually updating

00:08:53.660 --> 00:08:56.170
the game stage, frame by frame.

00:08:56.170 --> 00:08:58.730
And so it has to operate
pretty rapidly, again,

00:08:58.730 --> 00:09:01.260
20 or 30 frames per second.

00:09:01.260 --> 00:09:06.280
So if the game state is
kept in a separate database,

00:09:06.280 --> 00:09:09.700
again, probably
too much latency.

00:09:09.700 --> 00:09:12.760
So we chucked the
database and, instead, we

00:09:12.760 --> 00:09:17.420
kept the game state in memory
in our back-end servers.

00:09:17.420 --> 00:09:18.280
So?

00:09:18.280 --> 00:09:21.580
Now, imagine we've got this
game simulation running on one

00:09:21.580 --> 00:09:24.720
of those servers, say this one.

00:09:24.720 --> 00:09:28.790
Now, there's no guarantee
that it's the same server

00:09:28.790 --> 00:09:33.122
that all the players WebSockets
are connected to, right?

00:09:33.122 --> 00:09:35.330
The load balancer is just
going to connect the client

00:09:35.330 --> 00:09:37.310
to whatever it connects it to.

00:09:37.310 --> 00:09:40.370
So we were thinking, OK,
how do we deal with this?

00:09:40.370 --> 00:09:41.600
And there are a few ways.

00:09:41.600 --> 00:09:43.160
You can bypass
the load balancer.

00:09:43.160 --> 00:09:45.710
You can customize
the load balancer.

00:09:45.710 --> 00:09:49.860
Or you can let the servers
talk to each other,

00:09:49.860 --> 00:09:53.650
make our back-end servers parts
of an interconnected cluster,

00:09:53.650 --> 00:09:56.640
and so that they can forward
messages, back and forth,

00:09:56.640 --> 00:09:59.070
to wherever they need to be.

00:09:59.070 --> 00:10:04.010
So again, we were tinkering
with the app architecture,

00:10:04.010 --> 00:10:05.060
tinkering with it.

00:10:05.060 --> 00:10:07.010
And we ended up
landing on something

00:10:07.010 --> 00:10:08.905
that looks kind of like this.

00:10:08.905 --> 00:10:11.010
And we say, yeah,
OK, this'll work.

00:10:11.010 --> 00:10:12.120
This would work.

00:10:12.120 --> 00:10:15.020
This will work
for our tank game.

00:10:15.020 --> 00:10:21.320
And turns out that Elixir
and OTP support this kind

00:10:21.320 --> 00:10:23.780
of architecture quite well.

00:10:23.780 --> 00:10:27.780
They have stateful processes,
state kept in memory.

00:10:27.780 --> 00:10:30.660
There are nodes that
are interconnected,

00:10:30.660 --> 00:10:34.220
that can forward messages
back and forth to each other.

00:10:34.220 --> 00:10:37.610
This kind of real-time
distributed system

00:10:37.610 --> 00:10:40.850
is the kind of thing
that Elixir, that OTP,

00:10:40.850 --> 00:10:43.270
are particularly designed for.

00:10:43.270 --> 00:10:46.670
And so as we were learning OTP,
as we were learning Elixir,

00:10:46.670 --> 00:10:50.350
this is how we ended
up designing our app,

00:10:50.350 --> 00:10:55.870
so a little bit unconventional
compared with most web apps.

00:10:55.870 --> 00:10:57.320
So all this was going well.

00:10:57.320 --> 00:10:59.625
You know, we had our game.

00:10:59.625 --> 00:11:00.523
It was working.

00:11:00.523 --> 00:11:01.190
It was playable.

00:11:01.190 --> 00:11:03.780
We were having fun,
the four of us,

00:11:03.780 --> 00:11:08.475
and we decided, OK, let's
demo it to our meetup group.

00:11:08.475 --> 00:11:10.600
So let's [INAUDIBLE] and
let's get the whole meetup

00:11:10.600 --> 00:11:13.893
group together, and they
could all connect up,

00:11:13.893 --> 00:11:15.310
and we can play
the game together.

00:11:15.310 --> 00:11:19.390
And we can show them how it
works, how we developed it.

00:11:19.390 --> 00:11:21.070
So that was a great plan.

00:11:21.070 --> 00:11:24.190
Now we had to decide
how we were going

00:11:24.190 --> 00:11:30.710
to deploy the game so that
people could connect up to it.

00:11:30.710 --> 00:11:35.220
Well, this was 2015,
four years ago.

00:11:35.220 --> 00:11:38.540
And this is a time
that saw the emergence

00:11:38.540 --> 00:11:44.860
of a really interesting
deployment technology.

00:11:44.860 --> 00:11:49.120
Kubernetes was
interesting and important

00:11:49.120 --> 00:11:51.880
at the time it came
out because of the way

00:11:51.880 --> 00:11:55.450
that it oriented the
industry around container

00:11:55.450 --> 00:11:59.650
practices, the way that
it's codified the practices

00:11:59.650 --> 00:12:02.820
of container-based deployment.

00:12:02.820 --> 00:12:06.520
Technically, Kubernetes
is a very flexible system.

00:12:06.520 --> 00:12:09.580
And we'll talk a little bit
about that a little later.

00:12:09.580 --> 00:12:17.310
But in practice, its usage is
actually pretty opinionated.

00:12:17.310 --> 00:12:20.100
Conventionally, Kubernetes
wants your servers

00:12:20.100 --> 00:12:23.480
to be stateless,
no, not stateful.

00:12:23.480 --> 00:12:27.600
It wants them to be
transient, and not persistent.

00:12:27.600 --> 00:12:32.260
It wants them to be independent,
and not interconnected.

00:12:32.260 --> 00:12:34.920
This didn't match the way
that our app was designed.

00:12:37.850 --> 00:12:40.430
Yeah, you have Kubernetes
StatefulSets also.

00:12:40.430 --> 00:12:42.620
They were called
PetSets at the time.

00:12:42.620 --> 00:12:46.230
But even those didn't quite fit
with what we were trying to do.

00:12:46.230 --> 00:12:48.380
We were keeping
states in memory as

00:12:48.380 --> 00:12:51.330
these long-running processes.

00:12:51.330 --> 00:12:56.090
So when we tried to deploy our
tank game using Kubernetes,

00:12:56.090 --> 00:12:59.390
we ran afoul of some
of these opinions.

00:12:59.390 --> 00:13:04.378
And at the time, we said,
ugh, let's just punt on this.

00:13:04.378 --> 00:13:06.670
We just wanted to get something
working for our meetup,

00:13:06.670 --> 00:13:10.530
so we punted on it.

00:13:10.530 --> 00:13:14.130
But it does bring up a very
interesting and important

00:13:14.130 --> 00:13:16.350
question, and it's
something that we eventually

00:13:16.350 --> 00:13:20.670
came back to when we
started thinking about,

00:13:20.670 --> 00:13:24.330
how would we really deploy
this if it were a production

00:13:24.330 --> 00:13:26.650
application?

00:13:26.650 --> 00:13:31.570
We started asking
ourselves, is Kubernetes,

00:13:31.570 --> 00:13:33.850
even though it's
embodying what's

00:13:33.850 --> 00:13:37.240
now kind of becoming
cloud best practices,

00:13:37.240 --> 00:13:40.060
is Kubernetes just not the
right technology for deploying

00:13:40.060 --> 00:13:42.260
an app like this?

00:13:42.260 --> 00:13:46.670
Are containers just not
the right technology?

00:13:46.670 --> 00:13:51.740
Is the cloud itself, cloud best
practices, just not designed

00:13:51.740 --> 00:13:55.820
for apps like this?

00:13:55.820 --> 00:13:59.360
And we were not alone in
asking some of these questions.

00:13:59.360 --> 00:14:02.330
The Elixir and OTP
community, as a whole,

00:14:02.330 --> 00:14:05.830
has been debating this quite
a bit over the past few years.

00:14:05.830 --> 00:14:07.880
There's a feeling
that containers

00:14:07.880 --> 00:14:12.680
and conventional cloud practices
just don't fit with the way

00:14:12.680 --> 00:14:15.170
that our apps look.

00:14:15.170 --> 00:14:17.870
Because of this,
the OTP community

00:14:17.870 --> 00:14:21.480
has been kind of slow
in adopting the cloud.

00:14:21.480 --> 00:14:24.270
So later, when we came
back to this question,

00:14:24.270 --> 00:14:27.300
and we started
thinking, OK, if we

00:14:27.300 --> 00:14:30.120
wanted to deploy this
to say, Kubernetes

00:14:30.120 --> 00:14:32.360
Engine, what would we do?

00:14:32.360 --> 00:14:36.090
What should we do when our
app breaks the rules, when

00:14:36.090 --> 00:14:37.450
our platform breaks the rules?

00:14:40.560 --> 00:14:41.650
Can it be done?

00:14:41.650 --> 00:14:44.530
Can I deploy this
to GKE and actually

00:14:44.530 --> 00:14:47.300
have it work reasonably well?

00:14:47.300 --> 00:14:50.050
So let's take a closer
look at this app,

00:14:50.050 --> 00:14:54.083
and where does it not fit.

00:14:54.083 --> 00:14:55.625
Where does it not
fit with the cloud,

00:14:55.625 --> 00:14:58.540
and what can we do about it?

00:14:58.540 --> 00:15:01.980
Well, first, the workers in
our app are interconnected.

00:15:01.980 --> 00:15:05.940
They form a cluster, and they
have to talk to each other.

00:15:05.940 --> 00:15:08.400
So now, of course, normal
Kubernetes practice

00:15:08.400 --> 00:15:11.910
is for replicas to be
independent, right?

00:15:11.910 --> 00:15:16.230
But let's set normal practice
aside for the moment.

00:15:16.230 --> 00:15:18.930
If your workers need
to intercommunicate,

00:15:18.930 --> 00:15:20.670
what does that mean?

00:15:20.670 --> 00:15:23.310
Practically, that
means they need

00:15:23.310 --> 00:15:25.260
to know where each other are.

00:15:25.260 --> 00:15:28.120
You know, they need to know
how to reach each other.

00:15:28.120 --> 00:15:32.850
They need to have each other's
IP addresses, any credentials

00:15:32.850 --> 00:15:34.990
that they need to
talk to each other.

00:15:34.990 --> 00:15:38.280
There are ways to get
that sort of information.

00:15:38.280 --> 00:15:42.220
Most of us treat Kubernetes
as a deployment tool,

00:15:42.220 --> 00:15:44.505
but it really is, first
and foremost, an API.

00:15:47.100 --> 00:15:50.460
It's an API that you can
use to build deployment

00:15:50.460 --> 00:15:53.580
tools, to build ops tools.

00:15:53.580 --> 00:15:56.430
It provides all sorts
of useful information

00:15:56.430 --> 00:15:59.940
about the shape of
your deployment.

00:15:59.940 --> 00:16:04.505
And you can talk to this
API from within your app.

00:16:04.505 --> 00:16:06.130
So that might look
something like this.

00:16:06.130 --> 00:16:07.382
This is Elixir codes.

00:16:07.382 --> 00:16:09.090
It's adapted from our
tank game, so don't

00:16:09.090 --> 00:16:10.860
worry about the details here.

00:16:10.860 --> 00:16:13.560
I just want to point
out a couple of things.

00:16:13.560 --> 00:16:17.520
Kubernetes provides you with
your pod service account

00:16:17.520 --> 00:16:21.360
credentials, the credentials
you need to talk to other things

00:16:21.360 --> 00:16:24.610
in the Kubernetes cluster
and the Kubernetes ecosystem.

00:16:24.610 --> 00:16:26.290
These are available
in the file system.

00:16:26.290 --> 00:16:28.440
So we're setting the
path to those credentials

00:16:28.440 --> 00:16:30.720
here as a constant.

00:16:30.720 --> 00:16:34.120
Kubernetes DNS also
provides the domain name.

00:16:34.120 --> 00:16:36.570
It exposes the domain
name for itself,

00:16:36.570 --> 00:16:39.720
for its own API endpoint, so
we're setting that constant.

00:16:39.720 --> 00:16:41.160
And we can use that information.

00:16:41.160 --> 00:16:44.550
We have everything we need to
send requests to the Kubernetes

00:16:44.550 --> 00:16:47.920
API from within your app.

00:16:47.920 --> 00:16:50.250
So this code, for
example, it returns

00:16:50.250 --> 00:16:52.740
a list of the IP
addresses for all the pods

00:16:52.740 --> 00:16:55.050
in your deployments.

00:16:55.050 --> 00:16:56.820
So once you have
those addresses,

00:16:56.820 --> 00:16:59.830
pods can reach each other.

00:16:59.830 --> 00:17:04.260
So what if, as each pod in
your Kubernetes deployment

00:17:04.260 --> 00:17:06.640
spins up, as each
container spins up,

00:17:06.640 --> 00:17:10.140
we ran that code
during initialization?

00:17:10.140 --> 00:17:12.300
We contact the Kubernetes API.

00:17:12.300 --> 00:17:15.630
We get a list of all the other
pods running, a list of IP

00:17:15.630 --> 00:17:17.240
addresses.

00:17:17.240 --> 00:17:19.829
And then we can use that to
establish any connections

00:17:19.829 --> 00:17:25.210
that we need to add that
new worker to the cluster.

00:17:25.210 --> 00:17:28.790
We run all that
during initialization.

00:17:28.790 --> 00:17:31.190
Now, when a worker
is shutting down,

00:17:31.190 --> 00:17:33.540
we want to remove
it from the cluster.

00:17:33.540 --> 00:17:37.170
So we might need to do some
disconnection, some clean-up.

00:17:37.170 --> 00:17:40.670
And again, you can use tools
that Kubernetes gives you,

00:17:40.670 --> 00:17:42.530
such as lifecycle hooks.

00:17:42.530 --> 00:17:45.140
Well, many of you are
already familiar with this.

00:17:45.140 --> 00:17:48.740
You can configure your
pods in your containers,

00:17:48.740 --> 00:17:51.110
with your pods
with a PreStop hook

00:17:51.110 --> 00:17:55.580
that gets executed when
that pod is terminated.

00:17:55.580 --> 00:17:57.710
You can also trap
the term signal

00:17:57.710 --> 00:18:02.190
that sends to your container
when it gets terminated.

00:18:02.190 --> 00:18:05.240
So you have these initialization
and these shutdown

00:18:05.240 --> 00:18:08.780
hooks available, and
they can enable your app

00:18:08.780 --> 00:18:11.810
to maintain an
interconnected cluster,

00:18:11.810 --> 00:18:15.630
like our tank game needed.

00:18:15.630 --> 00:18:18.960
This is a pretty common use
case for Elixir developers,

00:18:18.960 --> 00:18:22.950
so the community has
developed a library for it.

00:18:22.950 --> 00:18:24.980
But even if you're not
an Elixir developer,

00:18:24.980 --> 00:18:29.750
and I know most of us are not,
if your app needs information

00:18:29.750 --> 00:18:32.030
about its own
deployment environments,

00:18:32.030 --> 00:18:35.350
just a little digging
into the Kubernetes API

00:18:35.350 --> 00:18:39.920
will yield a lot of
useful information.

00:18:39.920 --> 00:18:43.310
All right, so far,
pretty straightforward.

00:18:43.310 --> 00:18:47.000
What else is our app
doing differently?

00:18:47.000 --> 00:18:51.490
Well, our workers are hosting
these long-running processes,

00:18:51.490 --> 00:18:54.500
these long-running game
simulations that keep state.

00:18:54.500 --> 00:18:57.305
And not only keep state, they're
not just stateful processes,

00:18:57.305 --> 00:19:00.610
but they're keeping
state in memory.

00:19:00.610 --> 00:19:03.420
So why is this an issue?

00:19:03.420 --> 00:19:05.790
Well, most normal
deployment practices

00:19:05.790 --> 00:19:10.080
assume that you can bring
down a container at any time.

00:19:10.080 --> 00:19:11.940
You can bring down
a worker at anytime.

00:19:11.940 --> 00:19:15.210
They assume that back
ends are transient.

00:19:15.210 --> 00:19:17.940
They assume back
ends are stateless.

00:19:17.940 --> 00:19:19.950
And we're breaking those
rules because we're

00:19:19.950 --> 00:19:22.970
running these game
simulations in memory.

00:19:22.970 --> 00:19:26.235
And so when Kubernetes wants
to take down that container,

00:19:26.235 --> 00:19:29.500
say it wants to scale down, it
wants to do a rolling updates,

00:19:29.500 --> 00:19:30.250
we have a problem.

00:19:32.810 --> 00:19:36.380
Unless-- unless, what
if there was a way

00:19:36.380 --> 00:19:39.870
to move that running game
to a different worker?

00:19:43.020 --> 00:19:45.950
This is a technique known
as process migration,

00:19:45.950 --> 00:19:51.140
moving processes or state
from one node to another

00:19:51.140 --> 00:19:53.330
in the distributed
system in general,

00:19:53.330 --> 00:19:57.502
maybe within a cluster of
workers within Kubernetes.

00:19:57.502 --> 00:19:59.960
There are a number of different
forms of process migration.

00:19:59.960 --> 00:20:04.260
There's been a lot of research
done into it over the years.

00:20:04.260 --> 00:20:08.905
But in our case, it's fairly
simple, fairly straightforward.

00:20:08.905 --> 00:20:10.280
First thing you
need to do is you

00:20:10.280 --> 00:20:13.040
need to be able to
checkpoint a process.

00:20:13.040 --> 00:20:14.760
You need to be able to pause it.

00:20:14.760 --> 00:20:18.740
You need to capture its
state and serialize it.

00:20:18.740 --> 00:20:21.860
Now for a game simulation,
conceptually, this

00:20:21.860 --> 00:20:26.750
is not that difficult.
Your process is just

00:20:26.750 --> 00:20:30.170
this long-running process that's
updating the game world, frame

00:20:30.170 --> 00:20:32.975
by frame, so you just need to
capture one of those frames,

00:20:32.975 --> 00:20:35.700
you know, capture that
as a big data structure.

00:20:35.700 --> 00:20:39.050
And OTP actually provides
you with a number

00:20:39.050 --> 00:20:42.860
of tools that help with
that, capturing, managing,

00:20:42.860 --> 00:20:44.840
serializing state.

00:20:44.840 --> 00:20:47.960
So next thing is you
need to be able to move

00:20:47.960 --> 00:20:52.610
that state elsewhere and
continue running the process.

00:20:52.610 --> 00:20:54.080
One straightforward
way to do that

00:20:54.080 --> 00:20:57.120
is to use a
message-queuing service.

00:20:57.120 --> 00:20:58.950
So when a node is
about to go down--

00:20:58.950 --> 00:21:01.730
remember, we have those
Kubernetes shutdown hooks,

00:21:01.730 --> 00:21:03.020
right?

00:21:03.020 --> 00:21:04.330
So we use that.

00:21:04.330 --> 00:21:05.930
You pause the game--

00:21:05.930 --> 00:21:08.090
pause any games-- there
might be multiple games

00:21:08.090 --> 00:21:09.560
running on that node.

00:21:09.560 --> 00:21:12.020
We serialize their
state, and then we

00:21:12.020 --> 00:21:15.260
push them up to the queue.

00:21:15.260 --> 00:21:16.910
Then some other
node in the cluster

00:21:16.910 --> 00:21:19.610
can pull those [INAUDIBLE]
games down from the queue

00:21:19.610 --> 00:21:21.770
and resume running them.

00:21:21.770 --> 00:21:25.430
So now we've effectively
moved that game from one node

00:21:25.430 --> 00:21:27.223
to another node.

00:21:27.223 --> 00:21:28.640
And there are, of
course, a number

00:21:28.640 --> 00:21:32.090
of queuing systems available
for you to use those.

00:21:32.090 --> 00:21:33.520
There's RabbitMQ.

00:21:33.520 --> 00:21:35.370
There's Apache Kafka.

00:21:35.370 --> 00:21:37.310
If you're using Google
Cloud, you might just

00:21:37.310 --> 00:21:38.960
use Google Cloud Pub/Sub.

00:21:38.960 --> 00:21:42.550
And here's what that
would look like.

00:21:42.550 --> 00:21:47.890
You can create one topic
for process migration.

00:21:47.890 --> 00:21:50.450
You use a single
shared subscription

00:21:50.450 --> 00:21:53.750
for all the running
nodes to listen to.

00:21:53.750 --> 00:21:55.460
And when a container
is about to go down,

00:21:55.460 --> 00:21:59.150
you serialize games,
publish those game states

00:21:59.150 --> 00:22:02.030
up to that Pub/Sub topic.

00:22:02.030 --> 00:22:04.370
Each of those games will
then get pulled down

00:22:04.370 --> 00:22:07.400
by one of the other
nodes that's listening in

00:22:07.400 --> 00:22:08.780
on that subscription.

00:22:08.780 --> 00:22:11.390
Since we have a single
shared subscription,

00:22:11.390 --> 00:22:16.290
it will distribute games
among all the listeners.

00:22:16.290 --> 00:22:19.220
So if you want to implement
a system like this,

00:22:19.220 --> 00:22:22.860
Pub/Sub might be a
reasonable way to do it.

00:22:22.860 --> 00:22:26.090
There are a few things to
watch for, some things that we

00:22:26.090 --> 00:22:27.860
encountered.

00:22:27.860 --> 00:22:31.230
If you're familiar with
process migration in general,

00:22:31.230 --> 00:22:33.230
you probably know that
one of the big challenges

00:22:33.230 --> 00:22:36.740
is maintaining communication--

00:22:36.740 --> 00:22:37.880
maintaining communication.

00:22:37.880 --> 00:22:41.540
If you have a communication
channel with a process,

00:22:41.540 --> 00:22:45.220
say, you're a player
connected to a running game

00:22:45.220 --> 00:22:50.130
and that game moves to a
different node, what do you do?

00:22:50.130 --> 00:22:52.290
How do you reconnect
to that game?

00:22:52.290 --> 00:22:57.190
How do you find the new
locations for that process?

00:22:57.190 --> 00:23:00.300
One way to handle this
is to create and maintain

00:23:00.300 --> 00:23:06.120
a kind of address book,
mapping from a process ID,

00:23:06.120 --> 00:23:09.120
or a name, or some kind of
identifier for that process,

00:23:09.120 --> 00:23:13.890
for that game, say, to the
location where it can be found.

00:23:13.890 --> 00:23:18.877
In Elixir or OTP, this
is handled by a construct

00:23:18.877 --> 00:23:19.710
known as a registry.

00:23:22.650 --> 00:23:26.520
So that's something that
we use for our tank game.

00:23:26.520 --> 00:23:30.840
You could use a low-length
latency database like Redis.

00:23:30.840 --> 00:23:33.450
You could use some
distributed state mechanism,

00:23:33.450 --> 00:23:38.520
like a CRDT, anything that
you can use as a shared,

00:23:38.520 --> 00:23:41.380
consistent source of truth.

00:23:41.380 --> 00:23:44.340
So keep an address book so
that you can forward messages,

00:23:44.340 --> 00:23:47.160
so that you can
reconnect anything

00:23:47.160 --> 00:23:50.310
that needs to talk to those
processes that are moving.

00:23:50.310 --> 00:23:54.100
Another issue to consider
is load balancing.

00:23:54.100 --> 00:23:56.690
If you use Google Cloud
Pub/Sub, for example,

00:23:56.690 --> 00:23:59.980
it'll approximately load
balance across the listeners

00:23:59.980 --> 00:24:02.880
that are listening in on
this shared subscription.

00:24:02.880 --> 00:24:05.300
But there aren't
guarantees here.

00:24:05.300 --> 00:24:08.890
So if you want more
control, or maybe you

00:24:08.890 --> 00:24:12.850
need to distribute load
based on the actual CPU

00:24:12.850 --> 00:24:16.510
load on those workers,
so it'd probably

00:24:16.510 --> 00:24:18.940
be a good idea to do something
like monitoring load.

00:24:18.940 --> 00:24:21.700
You can use a product like
sector upgrade monitoring.

00:24:21.700 --> 00:24:24.910
There are third-party solutions
for that sort of thing.

00:24:24.910 --> 00:24:27.310
Based on that information,
then you can selectively

00:24:27.310 --> 00:24:29.650
enable or disable
listeners, or you can

00:24:29.650 --> 00:24:32.350
send [INAUDIBLE] as necessary.

00:24:32.350 --> 00:24:36.070
Some other queuing systems also
have some interesting features

00:24:36.070 --> 00:24:38.360
that can be helpful for
something like this.

00:24:38.360 --> 00:24:41.380
If you're using
RabbitMQ, for example,

00:24:41.380 --> 00:24:44.190
subscribers can
specify a priority.

00:24:44.190 --> 00:24:47.740
And so you can set a priority
based on, say, the load

00:24:47.740 --> 00:24:48.520
on your worker.

00:24:52.460 --> 00:24:55.370
One other item, depending
on the queuing system

00:24:55.370 --> 00:24:59.270
that you're using, it might be
possible under rare conditions

00:24:59.270 --> 00:25:02.360
for a message to get
delivered more than once.

00:25:02.360 --> 00:25:04.970
This is possible under
Google Cloud Pub/Sub,

00:25:04.970 --> 00:25:07.320
sometimes possible under
other queuing systems.

00:25:07.320 --> 00:25:10.430
So you might need a mechanism
to detect and resolve this case.

00:25:10.430 --> 00:25:12.590
And that's another case
where a process registry

00:25:12.590 --> 00:25:18.245
can help because it's a shared
source of truth for where

00:25:18.245 --> 00:25:20.300
a process needs to
be running, then

00:25:20.300 --> 00:25:25.160
you can use that to resolve who
wins, if multiple workers try

00:25:25.160 --> 00:25:28.050
to pull down a process.

00:25:28.050 --> 00:25:32.660
So just a few considerations
to be aware of.

00:25:32.660 --> 00:25:36.940
All right, so we've talked
about queuing systems as a way

00:25:36.940 --> 00:25:38.800
to do process migration.

00:25:38.800 --> 00:25:41.890
And they're a common way
for process migration,

00:25:41.890 --> 00:25:44.270
but they're not the only way.

00:25:44.270 --> 00:25:48.400
Our tank game was
written in OTP in Elixir,

00:25:48.400 --> 00:25:50.950
and that ecosystem
actually has access

00:25:50.950 --> 00:25:53.410
to a lot of really
interesting tools

00:25:53.410 --> 00:25:56.620
for writing distributed
applications, distributed

00:25:56.620 --> 00:25:58.420
systems.

00:25:58.420 --> 00:26:03.010
You could take advantage
of tools in the platform

00:26:03.010 --> 00:26:05.980
that you're running
for, the ecosystem,

00:26:05.980 --> 00:26:08.630
to build process
migration directly.

00:26:08.630 --> 00:26:12.310
So this is actually
what we ended up doing.

00:26:12.310 --> 00:26:14.680
We wrote our own process
migration in Elixir,

00:26:14.680 --> 00:26:18.430
and it turns out that there
are some nice libraries

00:26:18.430 --> 00:26:21.505
available in the
ecosystem as open source,

00:26:21.505 --> 00:26:25.840
so that did most of
what we needed to do.

00:26:25.840 --> 00:26:29.020
So again, most of us aren't
Elixir developers here,

00:26:29.020 --> 00:26:32.260
so I won't go down that rabbit
hole describing all of that.

00:26:32.260 --> 00:26:34.840
If you are interested
in a deeper dive in some

00:26:34.840 --> 00:26:37.650
of the things that
you can do, you

00:26:37.650 --> 00:26:40.150
can look for this talk, where
I did an ElixirConf last year.

00:26:40.150 --> 00:26:42.550
It's on YouTube.

00:26:42.550 --> 00:26:44.680
But in general, there
are multiple ways

00:26:44.680 --> 00:26:46.880
that you can implement
something like this.

00:26:46.880 --> 00:26:49.030
And there are some
trade-offs, of course,

00:26:49.030 --> 00:26:51.370
between different
implementations.

00:26:51.370 --> 00:26:54.580
There are performance versus
complexity trade-offs.

00:26:54.580 --> 00:26:57.020
One of the reasons
we rolled our own,

00:26:57.020 --> 00:27:00.160
and did that within
the Elixir ecosystem,

00:27:00.160 --> 00:27:04.600
we pass messages back and
forth as OTP messages.

00:27:04.600 --> 00:27:08.080
And one of the reasons we
did that was performance.

00:27:08.080 --> 00:27:10.870
We actually got really, really
incredible performance out

00:27:10.870 --> 00:27:11.740
of that.

00:27:11.740 --> 00:27:14.870
But it did mean that we
had to do some custom code.

00:27:14.870 --> 00:27:16.150
So there are some trade-offs.

00:27:16.150 --> 00:27:19.810
There are reliability concerns,
security considerations,

00:27:19.810 --> 00:27:21.530
that you might need
to think about.

00:27:21.530 --> 00:27:24.730
So you'll need to vet those
concerns for your app,

00:27:24.730 --> 00:27:27.790
depending on your
particular use case.

00:27:27.790 --> 00:27:29.590
But in general,
for our tank game,

00:27:29.590 --> 00:27:34.060
we found process migration
really interesting.

00:27:34.060 --> 00:27:36.520
It's a really interesting
thing because it actually

00:27:36.520 --> 00:27:39.880
let us have it both ways--

00:27:39.880 --> 00:27:41.190
let us have it both ways.

00:27:41.190 --> 00:27:45.400
It let us build an
unconventional app.

00:27:45.400 --> 00:27:48.370
It had a stateful back
ends, processes in memory,

00:27:48.370 --> 00:27:50.980
all the performance and
interactivity benefits

00:27:50.980 --> 00:27:52.630
that came with it.

00:27:52.630 --> 00:27:59.770
And it let us deploy that with
conventional cloud practices,

00:27:59.770 --> 00:28:03.070
with conventional Kubernetes,
and we got all the autoscaling,

00:28:03.070 --> 00:28:06.790
all the CI/CD benefits, all
the cloud kind of managements

00:28:06.790 --> 00:28:10.210
and ops benefits
that come with that.

00:28:10.210 --> 00:28:12.680
We got it both ways.

00:28:12.680 --> 00:28:15.550
So it was a really
interesting exercise for us,

00:28:15.550 --> 00:28:17.950
and we learned quite a bit.

00:28:17.950 --> 00:28:21.160
Of course, it is possible
that process migration,

00:28:21.160 --> 00:28:24.190
in particular, is not
appropriate for your app.

00:28:24.190 --> 00:28:26.500
Some workloads might
not lend themselves

00:28:26.500 --> 00:28:32.380
to pausing a running process,
snapshotting, and serializing,

00:28:32.380 --> 00:28:34.180
or performance
might be an issue.

00:28:34.180 --> 00:28:37.060
You know, for our tank game, we
got really good performance out

00:28:37.060 --> 00:28:39.880
of it, maybe a few
tens of milliseconds

00:28:39.880 --> 00:28:42.350
in order to migrate a game.

00:28:42.350 --> 00:28:45.070
So it was totally fine
for our application.

00:28:45.070 --> 00:28:48.400
People's internet connections
will lag for more than that.

00:28:48.400 --> 00:28:51.100
But maybe your app
has more stringent

00:28:51.100 --> 00:28:54.170
real-time requirements.

00:28:54.170 --> 00:28:57.070
So let's take a look
at some other ways

00:28:57.070 --> 00:28:59.170
that you can deal
with things like this,

00:28:59.170 --> 00:29:01.870
within memory states.

00:29:01.870 --> 00:29:05.710
Now if you're working
on a large-scale, say,

00:29:05.710 --> 00:29:09.520
multiplayer gaming system--

00:29:09.520 --> 00:29:12.640
professional gaming systems
often divide servers

00:29:12.640 --> 00:29:14.770
into separate pools.

00:29:14.770 --> 00:29:18.270
You have a set of
servers maybe that

00:29:18.270 --> 00:29:22.660
are for normal
web-like requests,

00:29:22.660 --> 00:29:26.540
so things like profile
pages, lobbies, and so forth.

00:29:26.540 --> 00:29:29.560
And these can be
managed and deployed

00:29:29.560 --> 00:29:34.120
with familiar techniques
like Kubernetes.

00:29:34.120 --> 00:29:39.340
But maybe game simulations,
maybe you need to do games,

00:29:39.340 --> 00:29:46.540
like, as long-running background
processes, states in memory,

00:29:46.540 --> 00:29:48.810
and maybe those things
can't be interrupted.

00:29:48.810 --> 00:29:52.690
So you might want to run those
on a separate pool of servers

00:29:52.690 --> 00:29:56.180
that are orchestrated
differently.

00:29:56.180 --> 00:29:58.450
So if you have some
servers that follow

00:29:58.450 --> 00:30:04.280
a different set of rules,
can you deal with that?

00:30:04.280 --> 00:30:05.940
What can you do about that?

00:30:05.940 --> 00:30:07.690
Well, fortunately, as
we mentioned before,

00:30:07.690 --> 00:30:10.330
Kubernetes is a highly
flexible system.

00:30:10.330 --> 00:30:13.510
It has opinions, but
it can be customized

00:30:13.510 --> 00:30:16.180
to implement different
sets of rules,

00:30:16.180 --> 00:30:20.740
even within the same
overall deployment.

00:30:20.740 --> 00:30:23.320
Agonase is a project that's
taken advantage of that.

00:30:23.320 --> 00:30:25.623
Some of you have
heard about this.

00:30:25.623 --> 00:30:27.040
We've talked about--
talk about it

00:30:27.040 --> 00:30:29.230
in some of the other sessions.

00:30:29.230 --> 00:30:33.850
It provides tools to manage
dedicated game servers,

00:30:33.850 --> 00:30:39.820
matchmaking some of the various
needs of a gaming system.

00:30:39.820 --> 00:30:43.600
It's being developed jointly by
Google Cloud and Ubisoft, which

00:30:43.600 --> 00:30:45.852
is a large game developer.

00:30:45.852 --> 00:30:47.560
So it's well worth
checking out if you're

00:30:47.560 --> 00:30:50.800
interested in working in
high-performance gaming,

00:30:50.800 --> 00:30:55.980
or anything else that
might be really similar.

00:31:00.340 --> 00:31:02.210
Sorry.

00:31:02.210 --> 00:31:05.440
OK, let's take a look
at one more example.

00:31:05.440 --> 00:31:09.370
One of the more interesting
features of Elixir and OTP

00:31:09.370 --> 00:31:12.640
is hot code upgrade--

00:31:12.640 --> 00:31:14.140
hot code upgrade.

00:31:14.140 --> 00:31:17.890
The OTP platform
actually has the ability

00:31:17.890 --> 00:31:22.240
to hot-upgrade your
code while it's running.

00:31:22.240 --> 00:31:25.990
You can have a code
base running code,

00:31:25.990 --> 00:31:29.065
and then it can seamlessly
translate to a new code base

00:31:29.065 --> 00:31:30.190
while that code is running.

00:31:30.190 --> 00:31:32.830
Does that seem far-fetched?

00:31:32.830 --> 00:31:37.090
Why would you ever need
to do something like that?

00:31:37.090 --> 00:31:40.530
Well, hot code upgrade
comes from OTP's roots

00:31:40.530 --> 00:31:43.935
as a telecom platform.

00:31:43.935 --> 00:31:46.960
It was originally developed
by Erlang back in the '80s,

00:31:46.960 --> 00:31:49.890
or by Ericsson back in the '80s.

00:31:49.890 --> 00:31:53.970
They developed the Erlang
language and the OTP platform.

00:31:53.970 --> 00:31:56.190
They developed it for
things like-- applications

00:31:56.190 --> 00:31:57.330
like switches.

00:31:57.330 --> 00:32:00.540
Say you have a switch that's
in the middle of running

00:32:00.540 --> 00:32:01.740
live phone calls.

00:32:01.740 --> 00:32:05.280
You have software
running on that switch.

00:32:05.280 --> 00:32:08.010
You can upgrade, if
you want to upgrade

00:32:08.010 --> 00:32:11.217
the software on that switch,
while those calls are running.

00:32:11.217 --> 00:32:13.050
You don't want to
disconnect your customers.

00:32:13.050 --> 00:32:15.015
You don't want to interrupt
those phone calls.

00:32:19.140 --> 00:32:21.330
So capabilities like
hot code upgrades

00:32:21.330 --> 00:32:23.910
were developed during this time.

00:32:23.910 --> 00:32:26.550
And it's a really
powerful capability.

00:32:26.550 --> 00:32:30.540
But it does seem to be kind
of contrary to what we usually

00:32:30.540 --> 00:32:31.500
do in the cloud, right?

00:32:31.500 --> 00:32:36.160
We kill old containers,
and we spin up new ones.

00:32:36.160 --> 00:32:38.820
So what if you wanted to
use something like hot code

00:32:38.820 --> 00:32:39.870
upgrade in the cloud?

00:32:39.870 --> 00:32:43.240
What if, you know, it seemed
appropriate for your app?

00:32:43.240 --> 00:32:47.280
And there are apps that could
take advantage of something

00:32:47.280 --> 00:32:49.150
like this.

00:32:49.150 --> 00:32:53.400
Well, here's how one service
is solving this problem.

00:32:53.400 --> 00:32:56.850
Gigalixir is a
platform as a service.

00:32:56.850 --> 00:33:00.000
It was designed especially
with Elixir and OTP

00:33:00.000 --> 00:33:01.600
applications in mind.

00:33:01.600 --> 00:33:04.560
It's not a Google product, but
it has some really interesting

00:33:04.560 --> 00:33:05.550
ideas.

00:33:05.550 --> 00:33:10.980
They realize that containers,
and kind of conventional cloud

00:33:10.980 --> 00:33:12.980
practices, contain
orchestration,

00:33:12.980 --> 00:33:17.640
is still valuable, still
usable for managing your cloud

00:33:17.640 --> 00:33:21.690
resources, and for doing
things like updating libraries,

00:33:21.690 --> 00:33:25.380
updating your OS, updating
your dependencies,

00:33:25.380 --> 00:33:29.450
But containers don't work
so well for hot upgrade,

00:33:29.450 --> 00:33:32.130
for updating software in-place.

00:33:32.130 --> 00:33:35.310
So Gigalixir developed
a hybrid approach,

00:33:35.310 --> 00:33:36.990
and it looks kind of like this.

00:33:36.990 --> 00:33:40.380
In traditional deployment,
you might create

00:33:40.380 --> 00:33:42.690
a build of your app in Docker.

00:33:42.690 --> 00:33:45.420
So you have all the
dependencies, the OS,

00:33:45.420 --> 00:33:47.790
and your app all in a
single Docker image.

00:33:47.790 --> 00:33:52.590
And this is what you deploy
to Kubernetes, to Cloud Run,

00:33:52.590 --> 00:33:54.900
for example.

00:33:54.900 --> 00:33:57.330
Now, Gigalixir modifies this.

00:33:57.330 --> 00:34:01.870
They separate your app from
the OS and dependencies.

00:34:01.870 --> 00:34:04.470
So they still build the OS
and dependencies in Docker,

00:34:04.470 --> 00:34:07.788
but the app is built
as a separate image.

00:34:07.788 --> 00:34:11.639
And it gets mounted into
the container as a volume.

00:34:11.639 --> 00:34:13.989
So since your app lives
on a separate volume,

00:34:13.989 --> 00:34:17.159
it has the ability to modify
itself on that volume,

00:34:17.159 --> 00:34:21.060
even though it's running
in the container.

00:34:21.060 --> 00:34:23.600
So now we can perform
hot code upgrade

00:34:23.600 --> 00:34:26.360
without taking down
that container.

00:34:26.360 --> 00:34:29.510
And then, of course, if we want
to make these larger but less

00:34:29.510 --> 00:34:32.540
frequent changes to the OS
or the dependency layer,

00:34:32.540 --> 00:34:35.690
these can be done using
familiar Kubernetes techniques,

00:34:35.690 --> 00:34:38.810
so again, a hybrid approach--

00:34:38.810 --> 00:34:40.030
hybrid approach.

00:34:40.030 --> 00:34:43.580
This is just another way that a
service has found creative ways

00:34:43.580 --> 00:34:47.389
to meet the needs of
unconventional apps,

00:34:47.389 --> 00:34:53.130
while continuing to fit into
kind of a cloud ecosystem.

00:34:53.130 --> 00:34:55.830
All right, what have
we learned here?

00:34:55.830 --> 00:34:57.490
What have we learned?

00:34:57.490 --> 00:35:01.050
Well, we've seen just a few,
just a few techniques that

00:35:01.050 --> 00:35:05.170
might be useful for deploying
unconventional apps,

00:35:05.170 --> 00:35:10.140
particularly stateful apps,
real-time interactive apps.

00:35:10.140 --> 00:35:13.980
We saw that you can use
the Kubernetes API to query

00:35:13.980 --> 00:35:15.880
your deployments environments.

00:35:15.880 --> 00:35:18.510
We saw how we can
use that information

00:35:18.510 --> 00:35:22.560
to establish communication
among your server replicas,

00:35:22.560 --> 00:35:25.440
so if your server replicas
need to forward messages

00:35:25.440 --> 00:35:27.330
to each other.

00:35:27.330 --> 00:35:30.480
We saw some ways that you
can use process migration

00:35:30.480 --> 00:35:33.330
to preserve server
side state, even

00:35:33.330 --> 00:35:37.020
to preserve running
server side processes

00:35:37.020 --> 00:35:40.740
when you redeploy your back end.

00:35:40.740 --> 00:35:44.760
We saw how some projects
are extending the Kubernetes

00:35:44.760 --> 00:35:48.930
infrastructure, and others
are using hybrid techniques

00:35:48.930 --> 00:35:52.800
to meet the needs of
highly interactive apps.

00:35:52.800 --> 00:35:54.510
In general, we've
seen that you don't

00:35:54.510 --> 00:35:59.250
need to throw away our tools
and our best practices.

00:35:59.250 --> 00:36:02.190
Often, we just need
to stretch them a bit.

00:36:02.190 --> 00:36:05.450
We just need to add
to them a little bit.

00:36:05.450 --> 00:36:08.110
And this is important.

00:36:08.110 --> 00:36:10.890
It's important to think
in terms of stretching

00:36:10.890 --> 00:36:14.400
because this process of
stretching our practices,

00:36:14.400 --> 00:36:19.680
that could be the basis of the
next generation of our Cloud

00:36:19.680 --> 00:36:22.500
Tools and our Cloud
Deployment practices,

00:36:22.500 --> 00:36:27.980
and maybe, maybe even the
evolution of the cloud itself.

00:36:27.980 --> 00:36:31.310
I mean, take
serverless, for example.

00:36:31.310 --> 00:36:35.346
There's a lot of talk about
serverless these days, right?

00:36:35.346 --> 00:36:37.890
There's a lot of talk, a
lot of excitement, maybe

00:36:37.890 --> 00:36:39.790
some skepticism.

00:36:39.790 --> 00:36:42.150
Today, serverless
is often equated

00:36:42.150 --> 00:36:44.115
with functions as a service.

00:36:44.115 --> 00:36:48.530
Now, functions are awesome for
some workloads, some workloads.

00:36:48.530 --> 00:36:49.930
But, I mean, let's be real.

00:36:49.930 --> 00:36:54.770
It won't work for our
tank game, all right.

00:36:54.770 --> 00:37:00.100
The functions are not the end
all and be all of serverless,

00:37:00.100 --> 00:37:01.480
and we've seen that.

00:37:01.480 --> 00:37:04.000
We've seen that during this
conference in Cloud Run.

00:37:04.000 --> 00:37:08.260
Cloud Run expands what's
possible under the umbrella

00:37:08.260 --> 00:37:09.911
of serverless.

00:37:09.911 --> 00:37:13.450
It expands it beyond functions,
just functions per se,

00:37:13.450 --> 00:37:16.400
just a bit, just a bit.

00:37:16.400 --> 00:37:20.090
What if we were to take
that a little bit further?

00:37:20.090 --> 00:37:23.740
What if we were to imagine
the future serverless where

00:37:23.740 --> 00:37:27.460
we could even have stateful
processes coexisting

00:37:27.460 --> 00:37:28.990
with stateless functions?

00:37:28.990 --> 00:37:31.340
What could that look like?

00:37:31.340 --> 00:37:33.690
What should it look like?

00:37:33.690 --> 00:37:35.700
Would it support
process migration?

00:37:35.700 --> 00:37:37.950
Would it support game servers?

00:37:37.950 --> 00:37:39.715
Would it support
hot code upgrade?

00:37:39.715 --> 00:37:42.830
Does it look totally different?

00:37:42.830 --> 00:37:44.320
Maybe it would be
a terrible idea.

00:37:44.320 --> 00:37:46.150
I don't know.

00:37:46.150 --> 00:37:48.030
I don't think anyone knows yet.

00:37:48.030 --> 00:37:50.640
But I think that's the fun part.

00:37:50.640 --> 00:37:53.810
That's the fun part of where
we're at right now in cloud.

00:37:53.810 --> 00:37:56.460
And it's the kind
of question that

00:37:56.460 --> 00:38:01.640
can, and should, be driven
by our unconventional apps,

00:38:01.640 --> 00:38:03.720
by apps like our
tank game, by apps

00:38:03.720 --> 00:38:07.260
that you are developing today.

00:38:07.260 --> 00:38:12.660
That's why I love being around
technologies like Elixir,

00:38:12.660 --> 00:38:14.850
and like OTP, and
the communities

00:38:14.850 --> 00:38:17.880
that are around those,
developers like you who

00:38:17.880 --> 00:38:22.830
are pushing the envelope,
who understand that it's OK,

00:38:22.830 --> 00:38:26.200
sometimes, to be unconventional.

00:38:26.200 --> 00:38:28.740
Because you are
the ones catalyzing

00:38:28.740 --> 00:38:31.980
those changes, the next
generation in cloud,

00:38:31.980 --> 00:38:37.140
coming up with the wild ideas,
stretching our best practices,

00:38:37.140 --> 00:38:38.790
and creating more
powerful tools,

00:38:38.790 --> 00:38:42.520
and moving the industry forward.

00:38:42.520 --> 00:38:45.010
Because we know that
what's conventional today

00:38:45.010 --> 00:38:47.240
might not be adequate
for tomorrow.

00:38:50.060 --> 00:38:53.230
So here we've talked
about just a few ideas,

00:38:53.230 --> 00:38:57.970
just a few techniques that might
be useful, or at least maybe

00:38:57.970 --> 00:39:00.160
interesting, maybe.

00:39:00.160 --> 00:39:03.290
I'm sure you have
your own ideas.

00:39:03.290 --> 00:39:04.370
That's the point here.

00:39:04.370 --> 00:39:07.420
I encourage you to come
up with your own ideas,

00:39:07.420 --> 00:39:09.980
to share them, get involved.

00:39:09.980 --> 00:39:11.890
This is your cloud.

00:39:11.890 --> 00:39:13.120
This is your cloud here.

00:39:13.120 --> 00:39:14.480
This is your community.

00:39:14.480 --> 00:39:16.405
I want to hear your ideas.

00:39:16.405 --> 00:39:18.280
Your colleagues here
want to hear your ideas.

00:39:18.280 --> 00:39:20.450
Google wants to hear your ideas.

00:39:20.450 --> 00:39:23.005
What will things look
like beyond that horizon?

00:39:26.750 --> 00:39:29.175
Let's move the cloud
forward together.

00:39:29.175 --> 00:39:30.050
Thank you for coming.

00:39:30.050 --> 00:39:33.400
[MUSIC PLAYING]

